    // representative photo — a real Rudier-cast Rodin bronze, not the specific forged pieces Hain produced
    body: `Guy Hain started out selling veterinary products before moving into the art trade, eventually opening a gallery in the Louvre des Antiquaires in Paris called Aux Ducs de Bourgogne. What set his fraud apart from ordinary art forgery was that he did not need to fake the sculpture itself — he only had to fake who cast it and when.

Rodin's bronzes were, by design, never meant to be one-of-a-kind. The sculptor authorized his foundries to produce multiple authentic casts from his original plaster models, and the Rudier family foundry was the workshop most closely associated with his work, casting pieces both during Rodin's lifetime, under the mark "Alexis Rudier," and for decades afterward under his son Georges. A lifetime Alexis Rudier cast has always commanded a steep premium over a later posthumous one, even though both come from the same mold and can be all but indistinguishable to the eye. Hain, who had cultivated a relationship with the Rudier foundry and gained access to its original molds and casting facilities — buying the Balland foundry in Luxeuil-les-Bains in 1990 and operating it with his wife — exploited exactly that gap. Investigators later found he had removed Georges Rudier's mark from freshly cast bronzes and stamped them instead with a forged version of his father Alexis's, manufacturing "lifetime" casts of works that had actually been produced decades after Rodin's death in 1917.

Hain did not stop at Rodin. French investigators who raided his operation found molds, models, and finished bronzes bearing the false attribution of nearly a hundred artists altogether, including Auguste Rodin, Antoine-Louis Barye, Camille Claudel, Jean-Baptiste Carpeaux, Aristide Maillol, and Pierre-Jules Mêne, alongside sculptors as far outside the Rodin circle as Constantin Brancusi, Alberto Giacometti, and Jean Arp. French police arrested Hain in January 1992 and seized roughly 20 metric tons of sculpture, molds, and casting equipment from his workshops — a haul investigative journalist Gilles Perrault later chronicled in a book titled, simply, "The $60 Million Scam," reflecting the scale investigators eventually put on the fraud (roughly 450 million French francs). Some 2,500 molds and models were confiscated outright, but experts estimated thousands more forged pieces — bearing false foundry marks that made posthumous casts pass as lifetime ones — had already reached private collectors, dealers, and public institutions around the world, most never definitively traced.

A French court in Lure convicted Hain in June 1997 and sentenced him to four years in prison; he served roughly 18 months before his release. The case became a landmark in French art-fraud law less for the size of the seizure than for what it proved: that a forger who never touched the original artist's hand — who simply misused a legitimate foundry's own casting rights and stamped the wrong name on an otherwise authentic bronze — could inflict damage on a market's confidence just as lasting as a forger who faked the sculpture from scratch. Years after Hain's conviction, dealers, auction houses, and the Rodin scholarly community were still working to identify and flag pieces that had passed through his hands, a reminder that in a market built on multiple authorized casts of the same work, the foundry mark can be worth more than the bronze itself.`,
