    title: 'Marc Hauser and the Faked Monkey Cognition Data That Ended a Harvard Career',
    slug: 'marc-hauser-harvard-cognition-fraud',
    author: 'ScamShield Editorial',
    tags: ['notorious', 'notorious-scammer', 'historical'],
    sourceUrl: 'https://retractionwatch.com/2012/09/05/former-harvard-psychology-prof-marc-hauser-committed-misconduct-in-four-nih-grants-ori/',
    coverImage: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Marc_Hauser,_2008_(cropped).jpg?width=1200',
    coverImageCredit: 'Photo: Dan Lurie (CC BY-SA 2.0)',
    coverImagePosition: 40,
    body: `Marc Hauser spent more than a decade as one of Harvard's most visible scientists, a psychology and biology professor whose research on animal cognition — what monkeys understand about numbers, rules, morality, and each other's intentions — regularly made its way into major journals, popular books like "Moral Minds" and "Wild Minds," and press coverage that treated him as a leading voice on the evolutionary roots of the human mind. Much of that reputation rested on experiments with cotton-top tamarins and rhesus macaques that were, at least in part, never run the way he described, and in some cases not run at all.

The first crack appeared years before Harvard acted. In a 1995 paper, Hauser reported that cotton-top tamarins could recognize themselves in mirrors — a striking claim, since mirror self-recognition had at that point been convincingly demonstrated only in a handful of species, none of them monkeys. Psychologist Gordon Gallup, who had pioneered the mirror test, asked to see the videotapes underlying the result and reported finding "not a thread of compelling evidence" for it; when he asked for additional footage, he was told the tapes had been stolen. Hauser's own lab was unable to replicate the finding in follow-up work, and the paper was formally retracted from the journal Cognition in 2002.

That retraction did not end his career — it took a graduate student in his own lab to do that. In 2007, members of Hauser's research team, reviewing videotapes of tamarins listening to sound patterns for a study on rule learning, found that the tape did not support the results Hauser had reported: coded rhesus monkey responses in the raw footage did not match the data written up for publication, particularly in ways that turned statistically insignificant results into significant ones. The students brought their concerns to the university, and Harvard opened a formal misconduct investigation later that year, eventually reviewing years of grant-funded work.

The university's internal investigation, completed in 2010 and only partly made public, found Hauser "solely responsible" for eight instances of scientific misconduct spanning three published papers and five unpublished studies. One of the flagged papers, the 2002 tamarin rule-learning study already retracted once, turned out to have fabricated half the data underlying its central graph: the monkeys had never actually been tested on the follow-up sound pattern the results claimed to describe. A second paper, published in Cognition in 2007, falsely claimed that two independent observers had coded the experimental videos when in fact only one had, alongside fabricated measures of how well those (nonexistent) independent coders agreed with each other. Video review also turned up a study for which Hauser's methods section claimed every trial had been recorded, when in reality only a fraction of the sessions — 30 tapes out of what should have been many more — actually existed.

Hauser resigned from Harvard in July 2011, weeks before an internal report would have gone further, telling colleagues he was pursuing "opportunities in the private sector" focused on education for at-risk youth. The federal Office of Research Integrity conducted its own independent review of the NIH-funded portions of his work and reached a parallel conclusion in September 2012, finding that Hauser had fabricated and falsified data across six instances tied to four separate federal grants — including inflating a reported result on rhesus monkeys reading human gestures from an actual 27-out-of-40 success rate to a reported 31 out of 40. Under a settlement with ORI, Hauser neither admitted nor denied the findings but accepted a three-year period, beginning in 2012, during which any federally funded research he touched required outside supervision and certification, and he was barred from serving on Public Health Service grant-review panels.

No criminal charges were ever filed; the case was, throughout, a matter of scientific and federal research-integrity process rather than a court proceeding, and it ended the way academic misconduct cases typically do — with retracted papers, a ruined research career, and no restitution to pay because no one had been asked for money in the first place. What Hauser's case cost instead was harder to quantify: years of subsequent research by other scientists that had cited his tamarin and monkey-cognition results as established groundwork, textbook chapters and popular-science claims about animal minds built on data his own lab's videotapes did not support, and a graduate program that had to reckon publicly with how long a star researcher's fabrications went unchallenged inside his own lab before students found the courage to report him.`,
