    coverImagePosition: 50,
    // representative photo — replace with a portrait if found
    body: `Jeffrey A. Levitt was a Baltimore lawyer and real estate investor — by his own later admission, a onetime slumlord — when he and his wife, Karol, put up a 258-acre parcel of land she owned as collateral and bought a majority interest in Old Court Savings and Loan, a small Pikesville, Maryland thrift, in September 1982. Old Court was insured not by the federal government but by the privately run Maryland Savings-Share Insurance Corporation (MSSIC), a system that let state-chartered thrifts advertise deposit guarantees similar to federal insurance while facing far less regulatory scrutiny. Levitt used that freedom to grow Old Court explosively, advertising interest rates well above what federally insured banks paid; deposits rose from roughly $86 million to $839 million in under three years, making it one of the fastest-growing thrifts in the country.

Levitt treated the thrift as a personal line of credit. State investigators later documented a pattern of self-dealing: he directed Old Court to lend money to corporations he personally owned, used inflated property appraisals to justify loans against real estate he controlled, and structured deals — in one instance, taking $3.6 million out of the thrift to buy a property with no note or loan documents on file, then selling it back to an Old Court subsidiary at a $500,000 profit — that funneled depositors' money into his own accounts. Prosecutors also said he diverted interest earned on mortgage escrow accounts the thrift held in trust for its customers between 1982 and 1985, bankrolling a lifestyle that reportedly included a fleet of Rolls-Royces and a private box at Baltimore Orioles games.

The scheme collapsed in May 1985, when rumors spread that MSSIC could not actually cover deposits at Old Court and a second troubled thrift, Merritt Commercial Savings and Loan. Depositors lined up for blocks outside branches trying to withdraw their savings, and the panic threatened to spread to every state-chartered thrift in Maryland. Governor Harry Hughes issued an emergency executive order capping withdrawals at $1,000 per month across the state's savings-and-loan system to halt the run, and the state ultimately had to step in to guarantee roughly $8 billion in deposits held across 102 Maryland thrifts.

Levitt was indicted on 25 counts of theft and misappropriation covering roughly $14.6 million in depositor funds. He pleaded guilty in May 1986, and a circuit court judge — calling it the largest theft in Maryland's history — sentenced him that July to 30 years in prison and required him to sign over his remaining assets to the state's deposit insurance fund. He served about six years before being paroled in 1993.

The Old Court collapse was the first major domino in Maryland's savings-and-loan crisis, and a preview, on a single-state scale, of the insider-looting pattern that would help sink hundreds of federally insured thrifts nationwide by the end of the decade: an owner treating a deposit-insured institution as his personal bank account, shielded from real scrutiny by depositors who assumed "insured" meant safe — right up until the money was already gone.`,
