    body: `Between 2009 and 2010, Nsikayomuzi Goodman Goqo ran an investment company called Ingede Mineral Holdings out of an office on Crooked Lane in Hillcrest, a suburb of Durban in South Africa's KwaZulu-Natal province. Goqo, an alumnus of the elite Michaelhouse boarding school, told prospective investors he was placing their money on the stock exchange and offering a guaranteed return of 30 percent every month for six months running — a rate that would have multiplied an investment several times over in half a year, and one no legitimate stock-market strategy could reliably deliver.

More than 4,000 people invested with Ingede, together handing over upward of R70 million, roughly $9 million at the exchange rates of the time. None of the money went anywhere near the stock exchange. Goqo spent it on himself instead, according to the evidence prosecutors later presented, including luxury cars, expensive watches, and designer clothing, with roughly R5 million of investor funds going toward vehicles alone. Like any scheme promising returns of that size, it depended on a constant stream of new deposits to keep paying the "interest" that kept existing investors from asking too many questions, and it left the great majority of the more than 4,000 investors with nothing when it finally gave out.

South African police arrested Goqo in January 2012, but the case against him took years to reach open court: his trial did not begin until 2018, six years after his arrest, and dragged on for years more before reaching a verdict. He was ultimately convicted on 4,111 separate counts of fraud and 4,111 counts of contravening South Africa's Banks Act, along with violations of the Financial Advisory and Intermediary Services Act — one charge for essentially every investor he had defrauded.

On September 26, 2025, the Pinetown Regional Court sentenced Goqo to an effective eight years in prison, running concurrently with separate five- and three-year sentences on the related charges. Goqo has since sought leave to appeal both his conviction and sentence, arguing among other things that the trial magistrate's ruling was colored by anger and that the court failed to credit him for R5 million that had been frozen in his bank account; the National Prosecuting Authority has said it will oppose the appeal, which is scheduled to be heard in the KwaZulu-Natal High Court in October 2026.

The Ingede case is as much a story about the machinery of justice as it is about the fraud itself: a scheme that ran for barely a year, in 2009 and 2010, took more than a decade and a half to work its way to a first sentence, with an appeal still pending well over sixteen years after the first investor handed over money. For the more than 4,000 people who invested, that gap between the fraud and any accountability for it is its own kind of cost, on top of the money itself that, for the large majority of them, was never coming back regardless of what any court eventually decided.`,
