    body: `Matthew Shane Perkins, of Washington County, Utah, presented himself to prospective investors as a "brilliant trader who consistently beat the market and rarely suffered losing days" — the kind of unbroken winning streak that should be a warning sign in itself, since no legitimate trading strategy avoids losses indefinitely. Between August 2023 and November 2025, according to the U.S. Attorney's Office for the District of Utah, that pitch helped Perkins and a business partner raise more than $89 million from over 200 investors nationwide.

Perkins operated a company called Forged Oak LLC and struck a business arrangement with the principal of RentDue Capital LLC, which recruited investors into three separate funds through social media, its own website, and in-person meetings. RentDue Capital forwarded the money it raised to Perkins to day-trade, and Perkins, in turn, fed RentDue Capital a stream of falsified daily trading results and altered brokerage statements designed to make the funds look consistently profitable. In early November 2025, prosecutors say, Perkins handed over a doctored brokerage statement claiming the funds held more than $133 million — at a moment when the real balance had fallen below $13 million. He had, in reality, lost tens of millions of dollars in day trading, and diverted millions more of investors' money toward a home down payment, a cabin, luxury vehicles, and a private airplane. Utah's Division of Securities separately filed a civil case naming RentDue Capital's principal, Jeffrey Jace Vernon, and another individual, Talease Perkins, as defendants; those civil allegations are unproven and distinct from the federal criminal case against Matthew Perkins himself.

Perkins pleaded guilty to one count of wire fraud on February 2, 2026, agreeing to pay restitution of $77,683,091.96 — the outstanding loss investigators calculated after accounting for the roughly $13 million still on hand — and to forfeit money, vehicles, a cabin, and an airplane traceable to the fraud. On September 2, 2026, U.S. District Judge Ann Marie McIff Allen sentenced him to 180 months (15 years) in federal prison, followed by three years of supervised release, and ordered him to pay the full restitution amount. "Perkins's elaborate scheme left a slew of victims in his wake," said Assistant U.S. Attorney Melissa Holyoak. "Many of the over 200 victims were everyday Americans who lost their life savings."

The Perkins case is a reminder that a fund's reported performance is only as trustworthy as the person supplying it. RentDue Capital's investors were relying on brokerage statements and trading summaries that came from Perkins himself rather than from any account statement generated directly by a custodian or registered broker-dealer — the same gap that lets a fabricated track record survive for years in other investment frauds. A day-trading operation claiming years of uninterrupted, market-beating returns is a claim any investor can and should verify against independent account records before wiring a single dollar, not one to accept on the strength of a spreadsheet the fund manager produced himself.`,
