    body: `Michael Kochen, 42, of Aventura, Florida, and Sandro Herek, 56, of Coral Springs, Florida, ran a durable medical equipment operation that prosecutors say treated elderly Medicare Advantage beneficiaries as a revenue stream rather than patients. According to the U.S. Attorney's Office for the Southern District of Florida, the two men orchestrated a telemarketing-driven scheme that generated approximately $35 million in false and fraudulent claims — and more than $19 million in actual payments — for back, knee, shoulder, and ankle braces that beneficiaries never needed and, in many cases, never wanted.

The scheme worked by paying illegal kickbacks to obtain doctors' signatures on orders for braces that were never based on a genuine medical examination, then using those orders to bill Medicare Advantage plans and ship equipment to seniors who had simply picked up the phone to a telemarketer. Prosecutors say Kochen and Herek pressured beneficiaries into accepting equipment through relentless, high-volume telemarketing campaigns, and that when Medicare's fraud-detection systems flagged and suspended one of their billing entities, the two men shifted the fraudulent billing to a different company under their control to keep the payments flowing — a pattern prosecutors said they repeated across multiple durable-medical-equipment companies they owned.

A federal jury in Miami convicted both men on December 22, 2025, following a trial rather than a guilty plea. Kochen was found guilty of conspiracy to commit health care and wire fraud, six counts of health care fraud, conspiracy to pay and receive health care kickbacks, and three counts of paying kickbacks; Herek was convicted of the same conspiracy counts along with one count of health care fraud and three counts of receiving kickbacks. On July 30, 2026, Senior U.S. District Judge Donald L. Graham sentenced Kochen to 204 months — 17 years — in federal prison, and Herek to 92 months. "Michael Kochen and Sandro Herek treated vulnerable seniors as profit centers and federal health care programs as personal bank accounts," said U.S. Attorney Jason A. Reding Quiñones for the Southern District of Florida, adding that prosecutors would pursue forfeiture and recovery of the pair's illicit gains. Because both men were convicted at trial rather than by guilty plea, either may still pursue an appeal; no such appeal had been publicly reported as of this writing, so their convictions and sentences should be understood as the trial court's judgment pending any further proceedings.

Medicare Advantage brace fraud follows a pattern regulators have flagged for years: a telemarketer's unsolicited call, a "free" brace offer that requires only a Medicare number to process, and a doctor's signature the beneficiary never actually obtained in person. A genuine need for orthopedic equipment is established by an in-person examination with a beneficiary's own physician, not by a phone call from a stranger — and anyone who receives medical equipment they didn't request should report it to Medicare rather than simply keep or discard it, since the beneficiary's own account is often billed regardless.`,
