    body: `Michael Scronic didn't run his hedge fund out of a Manhattan tower. He ran it out of Pound Ridge, a small, affluent town in Westchester County, New York, raising money almost entirely from people he already knew — neighbors, friends, and members of his own family, including his mother. Starting around 2010, Scronic solicited at least 42 of them to invest in the Scronic Macro Fund, an unregistered vehicle he told investors traded options on major stock indexes using a disciplined, risk-managed strategy with a long track record of steady gains.

The track record was fiction. According to the Securities and Exchange Commission's parallel civil case, Scronic reported positive returns in 21 of the 22 quarters between January 2012 and June 2017 — including a reported 13.4 percent gain in the fourth quarter of 2014 alone — while the fund's actual brokerage account lost money in 28 of its 29 quarters of real operation, racking up roughly $15.7 million in net trading losses before commissions. By June 30, 2017, Scronic was telling investors the fund held $21.8 million in assets; the brokerage account behind that number actually held just under $27,500. In the meantime, prosecutors said, Scronic drew roughly $500,000 a year in personal expenses out of the fund — $12,275 a month in rent on his Pound Ridge home, mortgage payments on a vacation property in Stratton, Vermont, membership dues at a beach club and a country club, and personal credit card bills — on top of the 1 percent management fee and 20 percent performance fee he charged on profits that, for most of the fund's life, didn't exist.

The scheme unraveled the way many affinity Ponzi schemes do: when enough investors asked to cash out at once, there was nowhere near enough real money left to pay them. The FBI arrested Scronic in October 2017, and he pleaded guilty in the Southern District of New York to securities fraud, admitting he had defrauded the fund's 45 investors of more than $22 million. On September 28, 2018, U.S. District Judge Cathy Seibel sentenced him to 96 months — eight years — in federal prison, followed by three years of supervised release, and ordered $22 million in restitution as part of his plea agreement. The SEC's parallel civil case sought a permanent injunction, disgorgement, and financial penalties on top of the criminal restitution.

Scronic's fund is a reminder that a Ponzi scheme doesn't need strangers or a slick marketing operation to work — the closer the fraud stays to people who already trust the person running it, family included, the less anyone feels the need to ask for proof that the quarterly statements match an account anyone outside the fund manager has actually looked at.`,
