    body: `Michael Rothenberg founded Rothenberg Ventures in San Francisco in 2012, marketing the firm as the venture-capital home for "millennial entrepreneurs" building "frontier technology" — later narrowing its focus to virtual and augmented reality. By the mid-2010s the firm managed close to 200 investors and more than $64 million in assets across several funds, and Rothenberg himself became a recognizable Silicon Valley figure for a reason that had nothing to do with returns: courtside Golden State Warriors seats, a private box at Formula E races, and lavish investor events that later earned him the nickname "Silicon Valley's party animal" in press coverage of his fall.

The Securities and Exchange Commission charged Rothenberg and his firm on August 20, 2018, alleging that over roughly three years he misappropriated millions of dollars from the funds he managed, including an estimated $7 million in fees beyond what the funds' governing documents allowed. According to the SEC's complaint, Rothenberg funneled investor money into River Studios, a virtual-reality production company he owned and had told investors was self-funded, and used a $2 million capital contribution from one investor to pay down his own personal debt rather than fund the business. Separately, prosecutors said he misrepresented the size of his funds and the fees he had collected to Silicon Valley Bank in order to secure a line of credit for his management company.

Rothenberg and his firm settled the SEC's civil case without admitting or denying the findings, agreeing to a bar from the securities industry with the right to reapply after five years; a federal court later ordered him to pay $18.8 million in disgorgement, roughly $3.7 million in prejudgment interest, and a $9 million civil penalty — about $31.4 million in total. The SEC's civil settlement did not end the case. The U.S. Attorney's Office for the Northern District of California separately indicted Rothenberg in June 2020 on 23 felony counts including wire fraud, bank fraud, money laundering, and making false statements to a bank. His first trial ended in a hung jury; a second trial concluded on November 16, 2023, when a federal jury in Oakland convicted him on 21 of the counts, exposing him to a potential sentence of decades in prison under federal guidelines. Sentencing was set for March 1, 2024, before U.S. District Judge Jon Tigar.

Rothenberg's case is a reminder that a venture fund's glamour can be part of the fraud rather than incidental to it: the courtside seats and marquee parties built the credibility that let him claim a VR studio was thriving on its own when investor money was propping it up, and let him tell a bank his funds held more assets and had collected fewer fees than they actually had. A pitch built on visible extravagance invites the assumption that the money behind it must be real — which is precisely the assumption Rothenberg spent years exploiting before a jury decided otherwise.`,
