    body: `Note: this profile describes a pending federal criminal prosecution. Malik Beasley, Ed Davis, and their co-defendants have not been convicted of anything described below, an indictment is only a set of charges rather than proof of guilt, and all defendants are presumed innocent unless and until proven guilty at trial.

By the time federal prosecutors in the Eastern District of New York unsealed charges against him in June 2026, Malik Beasley had already spent nine seasons in the NBA and earned close to $60 million in career salary. According to the indictment, that salary hadn't been enough to keep him ahead of his own gambling losses — and prosecutors say a group of associates saw that debt as a way in.

The scheme, as charged, worked through the modern engine of legal sports betting: player "prop bets," wagers on an individual athlete's statistics — points, rebounds, assists — rather than a game's final score. Prosecutors allege that during the 2023-24 season, while Beasley played for the Milwaukee Bucks, he agreed with former NBA player Ed Davis and several other co-conspirators to intentionally underperform in specific statistical categories in exchange for reduced gambling debts he owed Davis, so the group could place informed wagers against him. In one cited example, ahead of a January 26, 2024 game against the Cleveland Cavaliers, the group is accused of knowing in advance that Beasley intended to grab fewer rebounds than the sportsbook line of 3.5 — he finished with three — allowing conspirators to bet the "under" with inside knowledge no ordinary bettor had access to. Prosecutors say the group placed fraudulent wagers worth tens of thousands of dollars across at least three games that season. A wiretapped conversation cited in the indictment has Davis telling Beasley, "Only way you can beat Vegas is sports betting… We can make some good money" — a line prosecutors say captures the entire logic of the scheme in one sentence.

A federal grand jury indicted six defendants in total: Beasley, Davis, current NBA player agent Paolo Zamorano, and associates William Brown, Robert Gorodetsky, and Ernesto Plascencia, on charges including wire fraud conspiracy, bribery in sporting contests, honest services wire fraud conspiracy, and money laundering conspiracy. "As alleged, the defendants turned professional basketball into a criminal betting operation, bribing then-NBA player Malik Beasley to fix his performance in multiple games in order to place fraudulent wagers, enrich themselves and cheat legitimate sportsbooks," said U.S. Attorney Joseph Nocella Jr. in announcing the indictment. Davis, Brown, Gorodetsky, and Plascencia were taken into custody when the indictment was unsealed; Beasley and Zamorano were not arrested that day. The case followed an earlier phase of the same investigation, during which a sportsbook had flagged suspicious under-betting on Beasley's rebound totals in a January 2024 game — a probe in which Beasley's attorneys said in August 2025 that he was no longer a "target," before the grand jury returned the indictment charging him ten months later.

The Beasley and Davis case is a companion to two other 2025-2026 federal cases involving NBA figures — the prop-bet scheme that ended Toronto center Jontay Porter's career, and the mob-linked poker and insider-betting indictments against Chauncey Billups and Terry Rozier — that together illustrate the same underlying vulnerability: legal, app-based sports betting now lets anyone anywhere wager on the precise in-game statistics of a single athlete, and an athlete willing to nudge his own performance by a rebound or two, or simply share that he plans to, has effectively handed a bettor inside information no stock-market regulator would tolerate in a public company.`,
