    body: `Michael Lizaso Marasigan, of Dededo, Guam, helped run Hafa Adai Bingo, a charity bingo parlor in Tamuning operated under the Guam Shrine Club. Like similar charity-bingo operations across the country, Hafa Adai Bingo was pitched to patrons and regulators as a fundraising vehicle for a specific, sympathetic cause: covering airfare and travel costs so that sick and injured children from Guam, along with a parent or guardian, could fly to Shriners Children's Hospital in Honolulu for specialized medical care they couldn't get at home.

According to the U.S. Attorney's Office for the Districts of Guam and the Northern Mariana Islands, that charitable purpose was mostly a cover story. Between March 2015 and December 2021, Hafa Adai Bingo generated approximately $34 million in gross proceeds from bingo patrons who believed their money was funding children's medical travel. Prosecutors say Marasigan and co-defendants — including Guam Shrine Club officer Jose Arthur "Art" Chan Jr. and Christine Chan — instead diverted and laundered $10,750,804 of that money for purposes that had nothing to do with sick children, while the actual amount that reached the Shriners' Hawaii affiliate, Aloha Shriners, for its stated purpose dwindled to almost nothing: roughly $140,378 combined over 2015–2020, and zero dollars in 2021, the scheme's final year.

A federal jury convicted Marasigan on May 13, 2025, of conspiracy to operate an illegal gambling business, money laundering conspiracy, and conspiracy to commit wire fraud. Rather than face sentencing, Marasigan — who had been allowed to travel on a court-approved trip — never returned to Guam, becoming a fugitive. On May 18, 2026, a federal judge sentenced him in absentia to 262 months, nearly 22 years, in federal prison, along with $10,750,804 in restitution owed jointly with his co-defendants to Aloha Shriners, a separate $5,871,493 forfeiture money judgment, and a mandatory special assessment. The FBI added Marasigan to its Most Wanted Fraudsters list and is offering a $150,000 reward for information leading to his capture; he was reportedly spotted on security footage in Manila, Philippines, in March 2026, but as of this writing remains at large.

Marasigan's case is a reminder that a charity's cause doesn't have to be fake for the fundraising built on top of it to be fraudulent — Shriners Hospitals for Children is a real institution that genuinely treats children in need, which is exactly what made a bingo hall raising money in its name so effective at drawing in patrons who had no reason to suspect that all but a token fraction of what they contributed was being laundered away rather than spent on the mission printed on the flyer. For anyone giving through a specific local fundraiser rather than directly to a national charity, that distinction is one worth checking, since a familiar, trustworthy name attached to a local operation is no guarantee that the local operators are passing the money through as promised.`,
