    sourceUrl: 'https://www.secretservice.gov/newsroom/releases/2025/12/former-tallahassee-man-sentenced-computer-tech-support-fraud-scheme',
    body: `John Kuhlmann, 79, of Daytona Beach, Florida, wasn't the one calling elderly victims and telling them their computers were infected with viruses or that hackers had stolen their financial information — but according to federal prosecutors, his role was just as essential to a nationwide tech-support fraud scheme that took more than $3.5 million from over 300 elderly victims across the country.

Starting in November 2018, Kuhlmann opened two Florida corporations, Seminole Services, Inc. and QB Accounting Services, that outwardly presented themselves as legitimate computer-repair businesses. Neither was registered as a money transmitting business, as federal law requires of any company in the business of receiving and moving other people's money. That gap was the point: co-conspirators overseas ran the actual con, using alarming pop-ups and cold calls to convince elderly victims — some paying as much as $150,000 in a single instance — that they urgently needed to pay for "repair" or "protection" services. Once a victim paid, the money flowed into Kuhlmann's Florida business accounts before he wired it onward to conspirators in foreign countries, giving an overseas fraud operation a domestic-looking bank account to collect through and making the payments look, to any bank compliance system checking them, like ordinary U.S. business transactions rather than proceeds moving out of the country to fraud operators.

Kuhlmann was charged with operating an unlicensed money transmitting business and was sentenced in December 2025 in the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Florida to three years of supervised release and ordered to pay $1,005,313 in restitution to his victims. "This despicable fraudster facilitated the financial exploitation of several elderly victims, robbing them of not only their money, but of their dignity," said the U.S. Attorney's Office for the Northern District of Florida in announcing the sentence.

Kuhlmann's role illustrates a part of the tech-support scam pipeline that rarely makes headlines the way the overseas call centers do: someone still has to receive the money domestically and move it out of the country, and prosecutors increasingly charge that link in the chain — unlicensed money transmitting — even when the person filling it never spoke to a single victim directly. A U.S. business name and a U.S. bank account lent Kuhlmann's operation a veneer of legitimacy that a Florida-based "computer services" company shouldn't have needed if it were actually just doing computer repair, and that mismatch between a company's stated purpose and the money actually moving through it is often the first thing that draws regulatory attention.`,
